17
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:15,000
Sarko's exoskeleton is composed of over 1,300 separate parts,

18
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:19,000
made of everything from aluminum to steel to bronze.

19
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:23,000
The keys to its strength are electrically driven hydraulics,

20
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,000
operating much like muscles,

21
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:29,000
and steel cables subbing for tendons.

22
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:36,400
tendons. It magnifies the user's power, allowing our super soldier Rex

23
00:01:36,401 --> 00:01:42,740
Jamison to continuously lift 250 pounds and never break a sweat.

24
00:01:42,741 --> 00:01:47,300
I feel a tenth of the weight. It doesn't require a whole lot of effort. We've

25
00:01:47,301 --> 00:01:51,300
done that particular exercise basically till everyone got bored and walked away.

26
00:01:51,301 --> 00:02:01,840
The general objective is to do a lot of work or go fast

27
00:02:01,841 --> 00:02:04,400
with little exertion by the person

28
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:08,060
and little consumption of energy by the machine.

29
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:20,640
So greater strength with lower effort.

30
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,300
Though this complex apparatus weighs 150 pounds,

31
00:02:26,301 --> 00:02:29,300
it's as nimble as it is powerful.

32
00:02:29,301 --> 00:02:33,300
It allows the wearer full and free mobility.

33
00:02:33,301 --> 00:02:37,300
It follows you around without any problem.

34
00:02:37,301 --> 00:02:41,300
I correct in it the same way I would walking upstairs without it.

35
00:02:41,301 --> 00:02:49,300
It's effortless.

36
00:02:49,301 --> 00:02:52,800
This effortless movement is a result of sensors

37
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:56,300
that detect every action, no matter how subtle,

38
00:02:56,301 --> 00:02:57,300
that Rex is making.

39
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:02,300
Placed throughout the arms and legs,

40
00:03:02,301 --> 00:03:06,300
the sensors direct the suit to match every move of the user.

41
00:03:06,301 --> 00:03:08,300
The person has to do nothing more than command it

42
00:03:08,301 --> 00:03:12,300
with his movements, and the machine does all the work.

43
00:03:12,301 --> 00:03:15,300
It has to move like the person does and not fight

44
00:03:15,301 --> 00:03:17,300
as every movement.

45
00:03:17,301 --> 00:03:20,300
To create the synchronized spryness,

46
00:03:20,301 --> 00:03:23,300
Sarkos engineers started with a feat

47
00:03:23,301 --> 00:03:25,300
and worked their way up.

48
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:29,300
You can see down below he's got ankle actuators

49
00:03:29,301 --> 00:03:31,300
that move his foot up and down.

50
00:03:31,301 --> 00:03:35,300
He's got a rotator for his tibia and fibia, a knee joint.

51
00:03:35,301 --> 00:03:37,300
And we built the lorxtramity first

52
00:03:37,301 --> 00:03:39,300
that allowed the person to walk,

53
00:03:39,301 --> 00:03:43,300
get up and get down, walk up ladders, walk up ramps,

54
00:03:43,301 --> 00:03:45,300
kick balls, and all that sort of thing.

55
00:03:45,301 --> 00:03:50,300
The legs are designed to allow the user to walk normally,

56
00:03:50,301 --> 00:03:54,300
but still be able to support the exoskeleton's own weight

57
00:03:54,301 --> 00:03:57,300
in addition to a payload of over 200 pounds.

58
00:03:57,301 --> 00:04:00,300
He can even hold that weight on one leg.

59
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:05,300
When he moves around, this moves with his leg,

60
00:04:05,301 --> 00:04:08,300
but when he steps down, it actually tightens up

61
00:04:08,301 --> 00:04:10,300
and it holds the weight of the upper extremity

62
00:04:10,301 --> 00:04:13,300
and the back assembly, so that when he's standing

63
00:04:13,301 --> 00:04:15,300
in a full position powered up,

64
00:04:15,301 --> 00:04:17,300
he doesn't have much load on his shoulder.

65
00:04:17,301 --> 00:04:19,300
So the machine's carrying its weight

66
00:04:19,301 --> 00:04:22,300
and the load's weight, and he's carrying his own weight.

67
00:04:22,301 --> 00:04:25,300
The upper half of the exoskeleton

68
00:04:25,301 --> 00:04:29,300
works on the same principles as the lower,

69
00:04:29,301 --> 00:04:33,300
providing the arm's magnified power

70
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:37,300
while maintaining a full range of motion.

71
00:04:37,301 --> 00:04:39,300
The shoulder has a large range of motion

72
00:04:39,301 --> 00:04:41,300
much more than the related joint in your leg,

73
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:42,300
which is your hip.

74
00:04:42,301 --> 00:04:43,001
hip.

75
00:04:43,002 --> 00:04:48,300
So the shoulder and arm parts of the exoskeleton don't look like the lower ones.

76
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:52,300
So when he moves his elbow, the two muscles pull, the tendons pull,

77
00:04:52,301 --> 00:04:56,300
and he gets the range of motion that he'd like.

78
00:04:56,301 --> 00:05:02,300
Same thing happens with the wrist and with moving the shoulder in and out

79
00:05:02,301 --> 00:05:04,300
and with flexing the shoulder.

80
00:05:04,301 --> 00:05:09,300
Although the exoskeletons intricate hardware is now exposed,

81
00:05:09,301 --> 00:05:12,280
future plans call for encasing it in protective

82
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:17,540
of plating, made of anything from steel to Kevlar armor.

83
00:05:17,541 --> 00:05:21,340
It's too early for the Army to say when it will be ready to deploy.

84
00:05:21,341 --> 00:05:23,980
But once fully developed, the exoskeleton promises

85
00:05:23,981 --> 00:05:28,180
to revolutionize military operations.

86
00:05:28,181 --> 00:05:29,880
For starters, it will help free soldiers

87
00:05:29,881 --> 00:05:33,060
of the burdensome task of lugging around rucksacks,

88
00:05:33,061 --> 00:05:36,220
weighing as much as 150 pounds.

89
00:05:36,221 --> 00:05:38,460
Those rucks are extremely heavy.

90
00:05:38,461 --> 00:05:40,920
And it's really taking a toll on the soldiers.

91
00:05:40,921 --> 00:05:43,960
whether it's their physical ability to lift something

92
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:45,960
or being able to go long distances

93
00:05:45,961 --> 00:05:48,560
in order to search or destroy the enemy.

94
00:05:48,561 --> 00:05:50,800
I think a system like this will definitely

95
00:05:50,801 --> 00:05:53,940
help our war fighters into the future.

96
00:05:53,941 --> 00:05:56,860
But future applications of this super-powered exoskeleton

97
00:05:56,861 --> 00:05:59,100
aren't limited to the military.

98
00:05:59,101 --> 00:06:02,340
You can think of medical products that help cripple people walk,

99
00:06:02,341 --> 00:06:05,340
or you can keeping people safe from overworking themselves,

100
00:06:05,341 --> 00:06:08,420
working in warehouses, working in construction.

101
00:06:08,421 --> 00:06:10,820
As these engineers empower us with super strength,

102
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:12,920
by putting the machine around the man.

103
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:21,060
Others are trying to make us super by putting the machine in the man.

104
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,580
Such is the work of Professor Kevin Warwick

105
00:06:26,581 --> 00:06:29,160
and his colleagues at the University of Reading in England.

106
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:33,180
Could their pioneering work be the first step

107
00:06:33,181 --> 00:06:36,540
toward transforming us into super cyborgs like Robocop?

108
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:39,240
And Darth Vader?

109
00:06:39,241 --> 00:06:40,900
We're at a very exciting time.

110
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:44,480
being able particularly to link humans and technology together.

111
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:48,300
What we're trying to do is develop new ways and new interfaces

112
00:06:48,301 --> 00:06:52,180
by which humans can receive information from a machine

113
00:06:52,181 --> 00:06:54,300
and give information to a machine.

114
00:06:55,220 --> 00:07:00,060
To advance his research over the past decade, Warwick took a radical approach.

115
00:07:00,980 --> 00:07:03,960
During a five-year period, he acted as his own guinea pig,

116
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:07,820
carrying several surgically implanted computer chips in his body.

117
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:10,320
That's how he earned his mechanical moniker.

118
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:11,820
Mr. Cyborg.

119
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,460
The first tip that I had was in 1998.

120
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:18,760
I've in fact got one of these here.

121
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:23,500
This was a radio frequency identification device,

122
00:07:23,501 --> 00:07:25,700
and it was implanted in my left arm.

123
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:28,140
At the time, no one had done it before,

124
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:30,340
so we didn't know if there would be any problems,

125
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:31,540
any dangers with it,

126
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,600
and hence I went for it myself.

127
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,500
What it did was identify me to the computer in my building,

128
00:07:37,501 --> 00:07:40,640
so the computer opened doors for me, said hello.

129
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:42,480
switched on lights, that sort of thing.

130
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:44,600
But Warwick had much grander goals

131
00:07:44,601 --> 00:07:46,740
