If you want to look inside the wing,

247
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:13,180
you can see that we have separate chambers

248
00:16:13,181 --> 00:16:15,540
for the air to pressurize in.

249
00:16:16,140 --> 00:16:19,100
It's designed to actually make you fly

250
00:16:19,101 --> 00:16:20,460
when you exit the plane.

251
00:16:21,220 --> 00:16:23,420
The material inflates underneath your arms

252
00:16:23,421 --> 00:16:24,460
in between your legs.

253
00:16:24,560 --> 00:16:29,560
and creates basically an airfoil that you glide on.

254
00:16:29,561 --> 00:16:32,560
An airfoil is any surface or structure

255
00:16:32,561 --> 00:16:34,560
that provides an aircraft lift

256
00:16:34,561 --> 00:16:36,560
by creating reduced air pressure above it

257
00:16:36,561 --> 00:16:38,560
and increased pressure below.

258
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:43,560
We designed the wingsuit to be similar to a wing.

259
00:16:43,561 --> 00:16:46,560
Our body is the actual structural skeleton

260
00:16:46,561 --> 00:16:48,560
of the wing suit.

261
00:16:48,561 --> 00:16:51,560
As we descend, we are maneuvering our body

262
00:16:51,561 --> 00:16:53,560
in a way to turn the body

263
00:16:53,561 --> 00:16:56,320
turn this wingsuit through the air,

264
00:16:56,321 --> 00:17:00,200
similar to the way an aircraft flies.

265
00:17:00,201 --> 00:17:02,800
If you want to fly at that 200 mile per hour speed

266
00:17:02,801 --> 00:17:07,320
we mentioned earlier, try this wing suit on for size.

267
00:17:07,321 --> 00:17:10,060
Unlike the standard model, it has larger wings

268
00:17:10,061 --> 00:17:12,900
and hand grippers for the pilot to hold

269
00:17:12,901 --> 00:17:16,460
to enable him to control the angle of the wings.

270
00:17:16,461 --> 00:17:20,020
This model of suit is built for specifically speed.

271
00:17:20,021 --> 00:17:23,060
You'll notice that the wings extend past the fingertips,

272
00:17:23,061 --> 00:17:28,060
and pass the toes with the swept wing for speed.

273
00:17:33,060 --> 00:17:36,060
Some wing suits are tailor made for another performance attribute.

274
00:17:36,061 --> 00:17:39,060
No super flyer can do without maneuverability.

275
00:17:39,061 --> 00:17:42,060
Now the last suit we had on was made for speed.

276
00:17:42,061 --> 00:17:46,060
This one, in this particular design, is made for aerobatics.

277
00:17:46,061 --> 00:17:50,060
Notice the cut on the wing with nothing hanging below, no grippers,

278
00:17:50,061 --> 00:17:52,060
and the cut of the leg wing.

279
00:17:52,061 --> 00:17:53,960
of the leg wing.

280
00:17:53,961 --> 00:17:58,120
This suit is a shorter cut to allow for faster turns.

281
00:17:58,121 --> 00:17:59,500
Your hands are free.

282
00:17:59,501 --> 00:18:02,160
You can move a lot easier with this suit.

283
00:18:02,161 --> 00:18:04,500
But no matter how good the aerodynamic design

284
00:18:04,501 --> 00:18:07,180
of these suits, they don't do the flying.

285
00:18:07,181 --> 00:18:09,820
The pilot wearing them does.

286
00:18:09,821 --> 00:18:12,740
Creating different flight paths with the wing suit

287
00:18:12,741 --> 00:18:15,840
is as simple as tilting the shoulders

288
00:18:15,841 --> 00:18:18,700
or bringing an arm in.

289
00:18:18,701 --> 00:18:21,960
Arching will naturally make you fall faster,

290
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:25,160
But in the wingsuit, we tend to cup our body,

291
00:18:25,161 --> 00:18:29,060
creating the shape of a wing to generate more forward speed

292
00:18:29,061 --> 00:18:30,560
and create lift.

293
00:18:30,561 --> 00:18:34,160
And the tail works in the same effect as a bird does.

294
00:18:34,161 --> 00:18:38,260
We can twist our legs and create a pitch with the tail of the wing suit

295
00:18:38,261 --> 00:18:40,420
to also turn us.

296
00:18:40,421 --> 00:18:44,720
You can do everything from forward flips to hard banks

297
00:18:44,721 --> 00:18:46,420
to figure eights.

298
00:18:46,421 --> 00:18:50,180
You can actually roll over on your back, close your legs,

299
00:18:50,181 --> 00:18:52,040
have your legs come over the front, and be

300
00:18:52,060 --> 00:18:54,060
heading the other way within a matter of seconds.

301
00:18:54,920 --> 00:18:58,380
In the words of Buzz Lightyear, that's falling with style.

302
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:08,340
But no matter how masterful the control of Ed and other wingsuit flyers

303
00:19:08,341 --> 00:19:11,200
that can never steer clear of danger.

304
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:19,820
Skydiving in itself is dangerous.

305
00:19:19,821 --> 00:19:24,820
wearing the wing suit just adds a little bit more to it.

306
00:19:24,821 --> 00:19:28,820
And the danger starts the instant they leap from the plane.

307
00:19:28,821 --> 00:19:31,820
When you exit the plane with a wing suit on,

308
00:19:31,821 --> 00:19:34,820
you're actually using the speed of the aircraft

309
00:19:34,821 --> 00:19:37,820
to lift as you come out the door.

310
00:19:37,821 --> 00:19:40,820
So your airfoil as you come off of the plane

311
00:19:40,821 --> 00:19:43,820
can actually raise you above the plane

312
00:19:43,821 --> 00:19:46,820
if the plane is traveling at a fast enough speed.

313
00:19:46,821 --> 00:19:48,820
We want to watch the tail of the aircraft,

314
00:19:48,821 --> 00:19:51,160
of the aircraft because we can hit our head on it.

315
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:53,360
So what we do is we keep the wings contained

316
00:19:53,361 --> 00:19:57,320
until we see the actual obstruction, the horizontal stabilizer,

317
00:19:57,321 --> 00:19:59,660
in this case, pass above our head,

318
00:19:59,661 --> 00:20:02,860
and then we can expand the wings out.

319
00:20:02,861 --> 00:20:05,060
The dangers multiply when your starting point

320
00:20:05,061 --> 00:20:08,480
isn't an aircraft, but a cliff face.

321
00:20:08,481 --> 00:20:09,980
All right.

322
00:20:09,981 --> 00:20:12,980
Three, two, one, see ya.

323
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:30,681
Base jumping with a wing suit has developed into a different era now, so to speak, whereas years ago, people intended to fly the suit away from the wall as far as

324
00:20:30,682 --> 00:20:31,382
possible.

325
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:39,100
Today, they are flying the wingsuit next to the wall, virtually surfing the terrain of the wall.

326
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:48,800
As they descend lower in altitude, they carve away from the wall and try and get as far away from the wall at that lower altitude as possible.

327
00:20:48,820 --> 00:20:50,820
and then open a parachute.

328
00:20:50,821 --> 00:20:55,820
So what's next for wingsuit flyers?

329
00:20:55,821 --> 00:21:00,820
The future a wingsuit flight has barely been touched.

330
00:21:00,821 --> 00:21:04,820
There's so many different variations in materials,

331
00:21:04,821 --> 00:21:08,820
whether it be a harder material or a lighter material

332
00:21:08,821 --> 00:21:10,820
or something with less drag.

333
00:21:10,821 --> 00:21:15,820
Or maybe flying with rocket power, like Iron Man.

334
00:21:15,821 --> 00:21:18,820
Swiss birdman Eve Rossi did

335
00:21:18,821 --> 00:21:21,460
just that on September 26, 2008.

336
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,720
Using the same jet suit, he test piloted through the Alps.

337
00:21:28,860 --> 00:21:31,040
Rossi made history by leaping from a plane

338
00:21:31,041 --> 00:21:33,300
almost 9,000 feet above France

339
00:21:33,301 --> 00:21:36,580
and soaring across the English Channel to Great Britain.

340
00:21:42,820 --> 00:21:46,280
All he needs now is a secret identity and an evil nemesis.

341
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:53,060
If flying over the earth like Superman isn't what you're looking for,

342
00:21:53,061 --> 00:21:56,580
then maybe flying through the water like Aquaman is.

343
00:21:58,580 --> 00:22:04,500
In 2008, Speedo's new LZR racer swimsuit helped make Michael Phelps

344
00:22:04,501 --> 00:22:10,260
Superman in the water as he won a record-setting eight gold medals at the Beijing Olympics.

345
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:18,660
He and many other swimmers in Beijing got a strategic assist from suit designers.

346
00:22:18,820 --> 00:22:21,520
advancing our pursuit of aquatic superpowers.

347
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:29,200
This high-tech suit lowered racing times by nearly 2%.

348
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:33,240
A huge plus in a sport in which champions and the also-ranes

349
00:22:33,241 --> 00:22:35,900
can be separated by just hundreds of a second.

350
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:38,900
When I stand up on the block, I know I have the best suit on.

351
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:43,820
To know that you have a few extra hundreds or attempts

352
00:22:43,821 --> 00:22:46,140
to win a gold medal or to break a world record,

353
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:48,480
how can you argue with that?

354
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:55,820
Achieving these super speeds starts with a specially designed fabric.

355
00:22:55,821 --> 00:23:03,820
Speedo tested more than 60 different types of material

356
00:23:03,821 --> 00:23:07,820
before creating a highly compressive ultra-fine nylon thread

357
00:23:07,821 --> 00:23:10,820
that reduced drag in the water.

358
00:23:10,821 --> 00:23:13,820
With the help of these specialized strands,

359
00:23:13,821 --> 00:23:16,820
designers created highly compressive sections

360
00:23:16,821 --> 00:23:19,560
that were bonded together in a form-fitting design.

361
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:25,820
It's super, super tight, but it really feels like part of your body when you put it on.

362
00:23:26,120 --> 00:23:31,880
The suit squeezes the swimmer's body, holding their core ab and back muscles in a corset-like grip,

363
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:38,500
allowing the swimmer to maintain perfect body position without restricting the full range of motion that a swimmer needs

364
00:23:38,501 --> 00:23:40,380
to slice through the water.

365
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:43,960
But even with these high-tech suits,

366
00:23:46,820 --> 00:23:48,820
Humans are relatively poor swimmers.

367
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:53,700
We convert roughly only 3% of our kicks and strokes into forward motion,

368
00:23:54,700 --> 00:23:57,740
while dolphins and other sea swimmers convert nearly 80%.

369
00:24:01,700 --> 00:24:08,200
But Ted Chiamillow and his team of engineers in Nicholson, Georgia,

370
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:13,840
have designed a special swim fin that can make us all Superman in the water.

371
00:24:13,841 --> 00:24:18,840
I was drawn to moving quicker underwater.

372
00:24:18,841 --> 00:24:22,840
I've just been there, done that with scuba diving

373
00:24:22,841 --> 00:24:26,840
and wanted something a bit more exciting.

374
00:24:26,841 --> 00:24:29,840
This tool is going to make you feel what it feels like

375
00:24:29,841 --> 00:24:31,840
to cruise the ocean like a whale.

376
00:24:31,841 --> 00:24:34,840
It makes you a super swimmer.

377
00:24:34,841 --> 00:24:39,840
It enables you to reach speed at which you couldn't even think

378
00:24:39,841 --> 00:24:41,840
of going without it.

379
00:24:41,841 --> 00:24:49,840
They call it the Luna set, and it can propel a swimmer as fast as eight miles per hour,

380
00:24:49,841 --> 00:24:51,840
twice the speed of Michael Phelps.

381
00:24:55,840 --> 00:24:58,840
The lunaset produces 140 pounds of thrust,

382
00:24:58,841 --> 00:25:03,840
the equivalent of a 12-horsepower motor.

383
00:25:03,841 --> 00:25:08,840
A conventional swim fin produces only about 40 pounds of thrust.

384
00:25:08,841 --> 00:25:11,820
When you're swimming with a conventional, a conventional,

385
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:13,840
swim fin and you're in the power stroke,

386
00:25:13,841 --> 00:25:16,840
which is the down stroke, what you're doing is you're

387
00:25:16,841 --> 00:25:19,840
using the water pressure to flex the blade

388
00:25:19,841 --> 00:25:20,840
to the proper angle of attack.

389
00:25:20,841 --> 00:25:23,840
And what you end up with is a very small portion of the fin

390
00:25:23,841 --> 00:25:25,840
that's actually contributing to thrust,

391
00:25:25,841 --> 00:25:29,840
and a large portion of the fin is waste.

392
00:25:29,841 --> 00:25:32,840
Seeking a solution, Ted and his colleagues

393
00:25:32,841 --> 00:25:35,840
studied dolphins and whales, much like the Wright brothers

394
00:25:35,841 --> 00:25:37,840
studied birds.

395
00:25:37,841 --> 00:25:39,840
They were learning from nature's wisdom

396
00:25:39,841 --> 00:25:43,340
and trying to replicate what nature was doing with flight.

397
00:25:43,341 --> 00:25:47,340
And here I am a little over 100 years later.

398
00:25:47,341 --> 00:25:49,340
And I'm studying whales and dolphins,

399
00:25:49,341 --> 00:25:53,340
trying to figure out how to make a man swim faster.

400
00:25:53,341 --> 00:25:57,340
We're taking the geometry in nature's solutions,

401
00:25:57,341 --> 00:25:59,340
the whale swimming morphology.

402
00:25:59,341 --> 00:26:01,340
We're replicating that in the lunisodes head.

403
00:26:01,341 --> 00:26:04,840
And we're creating a propulsor, a swimming device

404
00:26:04,841 --> 00:26:07,340
that allows you to swim more efficiently.

405
00:26:07,341 --> 00:26:09,340
That efficiency hinges on the surface

406
00:26:09,341 --> 00:26:13,340
on the lunasette's ability to pivot instead of merely flexing,

407
00:26:13,341 --> 00:26:16,340
thereby better mimicking the fins of whales and dolphins.

408
00:26:16,341 --> 00:26:20,340
The lunasette is a very rigid piece of equipment

409
00:26:20,341 --> 00:26:23,340
compared to a traditional fin.

410
00:26:23,341 --> 00:26:26,340
It doesn't flex per se.

411
00:26:26,341 --> 00:26:29,340
It pivots on two pitch axles.

412
00:26:29,341 --> 00:26:33,340
And by doing this, it makes it that much more efficient.

413
00:26:33,341 --> 00:26:38,340
But even more crucial to the lunasette's design

414
00:26:38,341 --> 00:26:42,340
is its contoured wing-like shape.

415
00:26:42,341 --> 00:26:46,140
The lunisette has a foil that pitches about a pitch axle.

416
00:26:46,141 --> 00:26:49,880
And what you have, unlike the conventional swim fins,

417
00:26:49,881 --> 00:26:58,800
is an ideal angle of attack over the entire foil surface.

418
00:26:58,801 --> 00:27:01,000
The lunaset generates through the water,

419
00:27:01,001 --> 00:27:05,140
much like an aircraft propeller generates it in the air.

420
00:27:05,141 --> 00:27:08,340
The propeller blades wing-shaped design creates low air pressure,

421
00:27:08,341 --> 00:27:11,340
on one side of the blade and high pressure on the other

422
00:27:11,341 --> 00:27:14,340
to drive the plane forward.

423
00:27:14,341 --> 00:27:17,340
The lunaset acts the same way underwater,

424
00:27:17,341 --> 00:27:19,340
but instead of spinning like a propeller,

425
00:27:19,341 --> 00:27:21,340
it pivots up and down in a flapping motion

426
00:27:21,341 --> 00:27:24,340
to push the swimmer forward.

427
00:27:24,341 --> 00:27:27,340
As efficient as the lunaset is,

428
00:27:27,341 --> 00:27:29,340
the swimmer using it has to adopt an unusual motion

429
00:27:29,341 --> 00:27:32,340
to take full advantage of its design.

430
00:27:34,340 --> 00:27:36,340
To achieve the proper angle of attack

431
00:27:36,341 --> 00:27:37,340
and your super speed,

432
00:27:37,341 --> 00:27:41,340
You have to roll in a series of wave-like bends.

433
00:27:41,341 --> 00:27:44,340
Your core muscle trigger the movement,

434
00:27:44,341 --> 00:27:49,340
and then the rest of your body follows in an undulation.

435
00:27:49,341 --> 00:27:55,340
At first, it seems awkward, and then it becomes really quickly

436
00:27:55,341 --> 00:27:59,340
a natural movement, because that's what your body wants to do.

437
00:27:59,341 --> 00:28:03,340
The automatic response is trying to reproduce

438
00:28:03,341 --> 00:28:06,340
a marine mammal movement, which is a dolphin kick.

439
00:28:06,341 --> 00:28:07,340
a dolphin kick.

440
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:12,340
And once we start swimming as fast as the dolphins,

441
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:16,340
we just might be able to pull off one of their signature tricks.

442
00:28:18,340 --> 00:28:20,340
I think at the end of the day,

443
00:28:20,341 --> 00:28:23,340
we're going to have humans jumping out of the water,

444
00:28:23,341 --> 00:28:24,340
getting complete air.

445
00:28:27,340 --> 00:28:30,340
I think the vast majority of the people

446
00:28:30,341 --> 00:28:32,340
that are going to be lucky enough

447
00:28:32,341 --> 00:28:34,340
to get a lunatic set on their feet

448
00:28:34,341 --> 00:28:41,340
are going to experience something very different.

449
00:28:41,341 --> 00:28:44,340
This is as good as going to space.

450
00:28:44,341 --> 00:28:47,340
We have an environment here on Earth

451
00:28:47,341 --> 00:28:50,340
that is exactly like the outer space.

452
00:28:50,341 --> 00:28:54,340
And the beauty of it is that any of us can access it

453
00:28:54,341 --> 00:28:57,340
the way marine mammal access it.

454
00:28:57,341 --> 00:29:01,340
While some are mimicking nature to become super swimmers,

455
00:29:01,341 --> 00:29:04,340
others are using technology to take a giant

456
00:29:04,341 --> 00:29:06,340
for mankind.

457
00:29:27,074 --> 00:29:33,340
Inventor Jeff Jay is advancing our quest for superpowers by putting a new spring in our steps.

458
00:29:33,341 --> 00:29:34,340
our steps.

459
00:29:39,340 --> 00:29:42,340
His brainchild, velocity motion stilts,

460
00:29:42,341 --> 00:29:46,340
is the next best thing to Steve Austin's bionic legs.

461
00:29:46,341 --> 00:29:50,340
A velocity motion stilt is basically built for momentum.

462
00:29:50,341 --> 00:29:53,340
It accentuates what the human body already does

463
00:29:53,341 --> 00:29:54,340
in a direction.

464
00:29:54,341 --> 00:29:57,340
The velocity motion stilt basically is designed

465
00:29:57,341 --> 00:29:59,340
so that you can perform in it.

466
00:29:59,341 --> 00:30:02,340
Just start by, like, take, just put all your weight

467
00:30:02,341 --> 00:30:05,340
all your weight on the one spring, just keep it centered over top of this one spring.

468
00:30:06,840 --> 00:30:11,880
Jeff's clientele consists mainly of acrobats and stilt performers, like Lucas Mendonza,

469
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:15,940
who jump at the chance to pay the $2,000 price for one pair.

470
00:30:17,100 --> 00:30:23,400
It is really, really fun to jump out because, like, it looks like you on the moon, you know,

471
00:30:23,440 --> 00:30:25,280
that feeling like it's really good.

472
00:30:26,820 --> 00:30:31,440
Using the stilts, Jay has been able to sustain a speed of 23 miles an hour.

473
00:30:31,441 --> 00:30:35,320
the same pace as Olympic class sprinters covering the same distance.

474
00:30:36,940 --> 00:30:39,360
And he's leaped as high as 16 feet.

475
00:30:40,380 --> 00:30:43,900
This is definitely the Lamborghini of stilts right here.

476
00:30:44,520 --> 00:30:47,300
These high-flying stilts have only two moving parts.

477
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:52,520
But they masterfully harness the user's weight to generate the stilts power.

478
00:30:53,420 --> 00:30:56,480
The real energy comes from these springs that are located in the back.

479
00:30:56,481 --> 00:31:01,000
So if he bounces on two feet, you can see the flexion in the spring right in here.

480
00:31:01,440 --> 00:31:06,140
Okay, if he bounces on one foot, you can see how the spring loads a lot more.

481
00:31:06,141 --> 00:31:09,640
And the difference being is that all of his body weight now is on one spring,

482
00:31:09,641 --> 00:31:13,040
so it's easier to blow through the preload and get to the actual load.

483
00:31:13,041 --> 00:31:18,040
So if he goes back to two feet, now he has to bounce twice as hard to get the same spring.

484
00:31:18,041 --> 00:31:24,240
Much like a pogo stick, each still channels the weight of the user,

485
00:31:24,241 --> 00:31:27,240
pushing down the spring and loading it with potential energy.

486
00:31:27,241 --> 00:31:31,340
As the spring recoils, it releases that energy.

487
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:33,740
and pushes the user up.

488
00:31:33,741 --> 00:31:35,740
The more pressure that you put into the springs,

489
00:31:35,741 --> 00:31:39,140
the more return you're going to get out of the springs.

490
00:31:39,141 --> 00:31:42,040
And that returning pressure can be as much as 900 pounds

491
00:31:42,041 --> 00:31:44,440
per square inch.

492
00:31:44,441 --> 00:31:47,140
That's six times greater than the force of water spewing

493
00:31:47,141 --> 00:31:51,140
from a fire hose.

494
00:31:51,141 --> 00:31:55,440
